// A short reading guide for every existing rendered model.
export const modelHelp={
 units:'Read the ruler in metres, then compare the centimetre value. Changing units does not change the length.',
 scale:'Changing the exponent by one multiplies the size by ten. The two numbers describe the same length.',
 relationship:'The line shows how distance builds up. A faster speed makes the line steeper.',
 vector:'The dashed route goes across, then up. The solid arrow joins the same start and finish. Its length is the displacement size.',
 slope:'Read time across and position up. A steeper line means faster motion; a downward slope means negative velocity.',
 area:'The shaded area counts the position change. Area below zero is negative because the motion goes in the opposite direction.',
 uncertainty:'The centre mark is the estimate. The band reaches one stated uncertainty to each side; it is not a probability by itself.',
 radian:'The straight radius runs from the centre to the edge. The thick curved arc follows the edge. One radian makes these two lengths equal.',
 trig:'The circle has radius one. The endpoint’s horizontal value is cosine; its vertical value is sine. The angle is measured from rightward.',
 derivative:'The curved line gives the output. The short straight line shows its slope at the chosen input: how quickly the output changes nearby.',
 integral:'The shaded area under the line is the accumulated total. This model uses plain numbers with no physical units.',
 complex:'Across is the real part and up is the imaginary part. Their endpoint gives one complex number; its distance from zero is its magnitude.',
 matrix:'The thick input and thin output lines start at zero. This matrix doubles the horizontal part and keeps the vertical part.',
 motion:'Read time across and position up. The line begins at the starting position, which can be left of zero.',
 acceleration:'This is a velocity graph. Its slope is acceleration. Crossing zero means stopping for a moment before reversing direction.',
 projectile:'The curve is the ideal flight path from ground level, without air resistance. The dots mark equal time steps. The two axes use different drawing scales.',
 force:'The arrows are forces on one cart. Subtract the leftward force from the rightward force, then divide by mass to get acceleration.',
 efield:'Both sources stay at the labeled positions. Add their signed fields at the observer. The arrow shows direction only; read the numbers for strength.',
 rc:'The curve is the capacitor voltage over time. The dot marks the chosen time. One time constant leaves about 37% of the starting gap to the final voltage.',
 magnetic:'The rightward arrow is velocity. The other arrow is magnetic force, which bends the motion sideways. The dot-in-circle means the field points out of the screen.',
 damped:'The solid curve is position over time. The dashed curves bound the shrinking swings; they are not the exact position.',
 wavefield:'This is a snapshot of displacement along the medium. The marked place stays at x = 0.25 m while its displacement changes. Wave travel and local motion are different.',
 slit:'The centre is bright and the dips are dark. Height is intensity divided by centre intensity. A narrower slit spreads the pattern over wider angles.',
 'gas-isotherm':'Read volume across and pressure up. Each curve keeps amount and temperature fixed.',
 'thin-lens-image':'The lens is at zero. The object is on its left. A downward image is inverted. This shows image location and orientation; it is not a full ray trace.',
 'moving-clock':'The carried clock measures its own elapsed time, called proper time. The graph shows the laboratory time for that same interval at different speeds.',
 'decay-expectation':'The curve is an average expected count. Half remain after each half-life.',
 'signal-yield':'Read the bars in order: produced, chosen decay, then selected by the detector.',
 'fermi-occupation':'The curve gives the average occupancy of one complete electron state. At energy equal to the chemical potential, its value is one half.',
 'relative-starlight':'Flux is received power per area. The curve shows how the same source looks dimmer farther away.',
 'hr-diagram-summary':'Higher means greater total light output. Left means hotter. These broad star families are a guide, not measured dots. At equal temperature, a larger star sits higher.',
 'band-occupancy-diagram':'Read energy upward. The lower band is filled and the upper band is empty in this starting case. The 2 eV gap separates allowed bands; it does not count their electrons.',
 qwell:'Height is position probability density, not a particle path. Area over a region gives its probability. Relative phase changes how the two amplitudes add.',
 qgaussian:'The bell shows position probability density. Its centre stays still while it widens. A lower peak does not mean probability disappears; it spreads over a wider region.',
 qbarrier:'The curve gives the fraction of incoming probability flow that passes through the barrier. Height 1 means full transmission; it does not show a crossing time or path.',
 qradial:'The curve groups results by distance from the centre. Its peak is the most likely radius interval, not an electron orbit. Read the inside probability below separately.',
 q2spin:'This graph shows measurement chances. The preparation sets the state; the analyzer sets the direction measured. It is not a picture of a tiny spinning ball.',
 q2density:'This disk is a map of possible two-state preparations, not space. The edge is pure; the centre is an equal mixture. Purity measures how mixed the state is.',
 q2pair:'Each bar is a pair of measurement results. A joint preparation can change these pair chances while each side’s local chances stay the same.',
 q2levels:'The curve shows transfer into state 1 over time. Detuning means an energy mismatch, which can keep the largest transfer below one.',
 q3weak:'The solid curve is exact for this two-state model. The dashed estimate agrees near zero coupling and becomes less reliable as coupling grows.',
 q3trial:'The total curve adds motion energy and potential energy. Its lowest point is the best width in this chosen trial family, not proof of an exact ground state.',
 q3scatter:'Height gives scattering area per unit direction. The horizontal axis is the observation angle. It is not a particle path; the vertical scale changes with the settings.'
};
